# 014 — Private dynamic memory for programs — done

- Type: feature
- Priority: high
- Status: done (2026-09-19)
- Dependency: 013 (done)

## Problem and choice of the next step

After SMP and ring 3, a program has separate code, stack and data, but cannot
request memory at run time: BootInfo does not pass it a heap, and the ABI does not
contain allocate/free. The next step is kernel-managed private memory:
the basis for dynamic data structures, file loading and userspace libraries.

## Task statement

Add allocation of zeroed page blocks and freeing of a block by its
starting virtual address. The memory is accessible only to the calling process,
RW+NX, with an unmapped page after the block. Limits: 32 blocks and 16 MiB of live
data per process, within the existing shared 64 MiB runtime heap.
Errors in sizes, addresses, double frees and out-of-memory return
an error; they must not corrupt the kernel or already allocated blocks.

Page table updates must flush the local TLB before returning to
userspace. Tasks are still pinned to a CPU and single-threaded: one CR3 never executes
simultaneously on several CPUs. On exit, kill and fault, all blocks and tables
are freed. A failed allocation requires a full rollback.

Add a shared Rust wrapper that owns a block and use it in the standard app2
for the sprite buffer. This is a page-block API, not a malloc with small sub-allocations.

## Acceptance criteria

- [x] allocate/free syscalls, a documented ABI and limits.
- [x] Private zeroed RW+NX pages, guard pages, reuse of holes.
- [x] Freeing of tables/TLB; leak-free rollback on failure.
- [x] app2 uses a dynamic buffer; failure is handled without a panic.
- [x] Host tests for boundaries, fragmentation, privacy and rollback.
- [x] QEMU: parallel instances, allocate/free/reuse, invalid/double free,
      limits/OOM, NX/guard/use-after-free faults, exit/kill/fault without leaks.
- [x] Regressions of the earlier scenarios on 4 CPUs, the heap scenario also on 1 CPU.
- [x] README, the built usb_root and the task in issues-done/*.done updated.

## Implementation

- ABI: `SYSCALL_ALLOC=8`, size in arg1, result — an address or 0;
  `SYSCALL_FREE=9`, the exact block start address in arg1, result — 0 or
  usize::MAX. The limits and the page size are defined in `common/abi.rs`.
- `kernel/src/user_heap.rs` keeps the owners of physical blocks and the metadata
  in the kernel. First-fit reuses free virtual ranges.
  The arena occupies the last 32 MiB of the user window; after each block
  there is a guard page, and one more before the first block. Limit accounting is rounded up to 4 KiB.
- Mapping checks for overlaps in advance. An error at any stage rolls back
  the new PTEs and empty tables, keeping the old mappings. Unmap first
  detaches the tables, then flushes the TLB/paging-structure cache via CR3, and
  only after that frees the physical pages of the tables and data.
- Resources are owned by the Task and are freed automatically by the standard
  deferred cleanup mechanism after the CPU switches away from the finished process.
- The kernel GlobalAlloc disables local IRQs while the allocator lock is held.
  This removes a possible cycle: BSP holds the allocator → timer waits for the scheduler →
  AP holds the scheduler and waits for the allocator. The `heap` diagnostics are also serialized
  with system calls so that parallel activity does not look like a leak.
- `common/user_memory.rs`: `Pages::new`, a mutable slice, freeing in Drop.
  `app2` keeps a 64×64 sprite in a private buffer and uses it when drawing;
  lack of memory is handled with a message and a return to the OS.

## Verification

- Offline release build of the kernel, three applications and the bootloader — no warnings;
  the built `usb_root/` updated.
- 21 host tests: the earlier ELF/RTC/scheduler checks and new checks of privacy,
  zeroing/reuse, guard pages, sizes/overflows/quotas, invalid/double free,
  atomic failure on overlap and freeing of empty tables. Artificial
  exhaustion of tables after a partial mapping checks the rollback and a successful
  subsequent allocation, repeated three times.
- QEMU 4 CPUs: `normal`, `memory`, `busy`, `smp`, `isolation`, `heap` passed.
  The standard app2 confirms use of the private buffer, keeps drawing
  and exits correctly via Esc/Drop.
- New `heap` scenario: four instances on different CPUs continuously check
  different data patterns at identical virtual addresses; in parallel they call
  alloc/free, and the shell runs the heap command. All instances and the clock keep running.
- Checked: 32 blocks, the 16 MiB quota, zero/overflowing sizes, an address inside
  a block, double free and reading a freed buffer via a syscall.
  Twenty allocate/free cycles across 2 MiB boundaries return usage to the exact
  pre-test value while the process itself is still alive: the tables are freed too.
- Executing a heap page gives #PF with error=0x15; writing to a guard page or an already
  freed page gives #PF with error=0x6. Only the offending PID is terminated,
  the clock keeps running, outstanding allocations and tables are fully freed.
- For OOM, four clients are started beforehand: two hold 16 MiB each, subsequent
  requests get 0 without changing heap usage. After one owner is killed,
  the previously rejected allocation succeeds. A return to the initial usage was verified.
- QEMU 1 CPU: `smp`, `isolation`, `heap` passed separately. 9 QEMU scenarios in total.
- `git diff --check`, Python and build script syntax — no errors.

Logs: `/tmp/mind-core-<suite>-<N>cpu.log`. Reproduction commands are in the README.
`tests/heap_app.rs` is used only in the temporary FAT copy of the test VM.

## Stage boundaries

Memory is allocated eagerly, in whole pages, from the shared 64 MiB runtime arena.
Each block currently requires a contiguous physical region: failure due to
fragmentation is possible. There is no malloc/Rust GlobalAlloc for small userspace objects and no demand
paging. The guard only protects the unmapped page, not an overflow within a mapped
page. Address reuse does not provide temporal memory safety.
The local TLB flush is correct with the current pinned single-threaded processes;
shared address spaces on several CPUs will require a TLB shootdown.

## Audit (2026-10-04)

Was partial: the record claimed host tests for the user heap that never existed. Fixed in the audit commit: `user_heap::tests` (hole reuse, block and byte limits including retained memory, double and interior free, shareability of own blocks only, unmapping by node, detach). Rollback after page-table exhaustion is still covered only by the `memory` suite. Corrections: `common/user_memory.rs` is now `libmind/src/mem.rs`; the heap window is `USER_HEAP`..`USER_END`.
